{\rtlch\afs20\ltrch\hich\af5\afs20\dbch\afs20\loch\f5\fs20\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\ltrpar{\cf0\rtlch\afs20\ltrch\f5\fs20
I mapped pots, buttons, the 3 FSRs, including the 2 ribbon pots, and the LEDs. Some notes, starting with the LEDs. The LEDs must be connected to pins that are PWM capable. They cannot be on pin D13, which, on the Nano 33 IoT, controls the built-in LED. Therefore, the LEDs are connected as follows: D9, D10, D11, D12. On the Nano, D12 may not be a PWM, but on the Nano 33, it appears to be. The FSRs: there are 3. One is connected directly to the board, at A6. The others are on Mux2, an 8 channel Mux, 74HC4051, at positions Y4 and Y5. The ribbon pots get voltage, which I'm measuring at 2.32v, on their outer pins, and send the result voltage back to Mux2 at positions Y2, and Y3. The buttons are all functioning, so we'll work on them later. The FSRs are also reporting values. One of the ribbons is NOT reporting values, though it IS putting out voltage at Y3. Y2 and its ribbon is reporting. The code has these devices now mapped to CC # 16-19.  This may change later, and is not an issue.  I'm uploading the sketch. I have not worked on the tabs Controller.cpp, or Controller.h. Perhaps you can look into this to bring it into alignment with the changes made in the main sketch. LED behavior is not correct. When tapping the FSRs, including the directly connected one, one of the leds lights up. The idea is for each control to light its own LED. The directly connected one need not light an LED. Here's the main sketch:}
